---
import { getCollection, render } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Logo from '../components/Logo.astro';
import Wordmark from '../components/Wordmark.astro';

const entries = await getCollection('docs', ({ data }) => !data.draft);

const sections = await Promise.all(entries.map((e) => render(e)));

// Sidebar: one group per `#` part of the README (Manifesto, Framework,
// Using talaia, Contributing), each listing its `##` sections. Derived
// from the rendered headings, so renaming a section never breaks the
// grouping; only a `##` before the first `#` would end up ungrouped.
const toc = [] as { label: string; anchor: string; items: { anchor: string; title: string }[] }[];
for (const { headings } of sections) {
  for (const h of headings) {
    if (h.slug === 'footnote-label') continue;   // the footnotes' own (hidden) h2, not a section
    if (h.depth === 1) toc.push({ label: h.text, anchor: h.slug, items: [] });
    else if (h.depth === 2 && toc.length) toc[toc.length - 1].items.push({ anchor: h.slug, title: h.text });
  }
}

// Where the Companies form sends: a Formspree form, which mails it on.
const contact = 'https://formspree.io/f/xdeaqlnb';
---
<Base
  title="talaia — human in charge"
  description="A human-in-charge accountability standard for work done with AI: a certificate a person signs, and a method (Brief → Build → Watch → Sign) that makes the signature honest."
  toc={toc}
>
  <header class="hero" slot="hero">
    <div class="hero-in">
      <div class="lockup"><Logo size={30} /><Wordmark /></div>
      <h1 class="tagline">You direct. The AI builds.<br />Your name answers for it.</h1>
      <p class="hero-sub">Talaia is a human-in-charge standard for work done with AI, and
        its whole claim fits in one line: the machine may produce the work;
        only a person can answer for it. It gives that person a certificate to sign (under a
        published post, at the foot of a report, in a commit) and a
        method that makes the signature honest.</p>
      <p class="hero-cta">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={`#${toc[0]?.anchor}`}>Read the manifesto</a>
        <a class="btn" href={`#${toc[4]?.anchor}`}>Apply it, solo or as a team</a>
        <a class="btn" href="#companies">Bring talaia to your company</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <article class="readme">
    {sections.map(({ Content }) => <Content />)}
  </article>
  <!-- Companies: the one body written here and not in the README, so
       the standard on GitHub and in llms.txt carries no commercial part. -->
  <section id="companies" class="co" aria-label="Companies">
    <p class="co-over">For companies</p>
    <h2 class="co-title">Every use of AI in the company, with a person in charge of it.</h2>
    <p class="co-lede">Your teams already work with AI: in code, in reports, in processes
      that run on their own. What a customer, an auditor or your own board will ask is
      not whether AI was used. It is who decided it should be, who understands what it
      does, and who answers when it fails. <span class="wm">talaia</span> gives the company
      that answer: values held across the organization, a way of working that keeps
      judgment with people, and a signature that shows it in every piece of work.</p>

    <h3>What the company adopts</h3>
    <p>Not a tool, and not a new process. A position on AI, held by the whole
      organization before any tool is chosen.</p>
    <ul class="co-rows">
      <li><strong>The manifesto</strong> The company's values on AI: human judgment over
        machine autonomy, a person who answers over a system with no owner,
        understanding what goes out over accepting what works, and knowing when not to
        use it.</li>
      <li><strong>Human-in-charge</strong> Every use of AI has a named person in charge:
        someone who decided it, designed it, understands it and answers for it. That
        includes the automations nobody watches: the person answers for the system, not
        for each thing it produces.</li>
      <li><strong>The framework</strong> The line between what may be delegated and what
        must stay human. The design written once per system, then Brief, Build, Watch,
        Sign for each piece of work, under the process you already have.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>One practice, three directions</h3>
    <p>The Certificate of Understanding is how all of it shows: the same two clauses a
      single person signs. Adopted by a company, it becomes a good practice that travels
      with the work.</p>
    <ul class="co-rows">
      <li><strong>Inside the company</strong> Every piece of work done with AI (code, a
        report, a post, an automation) carries the name of a person who understands it
        and answers for it. The names accumulate as the work goes up: the author, the
        reviewer, the board.</li>
      <li><strong>To your customers</strong> Deliver the certificate as a seal on what
        you hand over: an AI helped make this, and a named person stands behind it.</li>
      <li><strong>From your distributors</strong> Require it of those whose work you
        receive, the way you already require any other standard of quality.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Services for adopting <span class="wm">talaia</span></h3>
    <ul class="co-cards">
      <li><strong>Training</strong> For everyone who works with AI, not only engineers:
        why the person stays above the tool, how to brief it, how to read what comes
        back, and when not to sign. For those who own an automation, how to answer for
        a system.</li>
      <li><strong>Audit</strong> An independent reading of where you stand today: where
        AI is used, who is in charge of each use, which work carries a signature, which
        does not, and what stands in the way.</li>
      <li><strong>Consulting</strong> The framework fitted to how you already work
        (your processes, your tools, your definition of done) and a clear line on what
        is handed to the machine and what is not. Nothing you have is replaced.</li>
      <li><strong>Official certification</strong> The standard is open
        and free: any company can adopt <span class="wm">talaia</span> and say so, on its
        own word. Official certification is ours: <span class="wm">talaia</span> checks how
        the company works (where it uses AI, who is in charge of each use, how the work
        is signed) and puts its name behind the result, so that your customers do not
        have to take your word for it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Engineering services</h3>
    <p>Beyond <span class="wm">talaia</span> itself, we build, the way the standard asks:
      the design written before the first line, a person in charge of every use of AI,
      and the work read in full and signed before it is handed over.</p>
    <ul class="co-cards">
      <li><strong>AI projects</strong> Agents, automations, document and data pipelines,
        put to work inside your business, each with a person in your company in charge of
        it who can explain how it works. Where AI is not the right tool, we say so.</li>
      <li><strong>Software engineering</strong> Products and platforms built and
        maintained with AI-assisted engineering: designed by people, built inside
        written rules, and understood by those who will answer for them.</li>
      <li><strong>Process optimization</strong> Your processes reviewed end to end: what
        to automate, what to hand to AI, what to leave as it is, and who stays in charge
        of each step.</li>
      <li><strong>Forward deployed engineers</strong> Our engineers inside your teams,
        working in your own repositories, pipelines and processes, the 
        <span class="wm"> talaia</span> way, until it runs without us.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="co-cta">
      <h3>Bring <span class="wm">talaia</span> to your company</h3>
      <form class="co-form" action={contact} method="POST">
        <label>Name <input name="name" autocomplete="name" /></label>
        <label>Company <input name="company" autocomplete="organization" /></label>
        <label>Email <input type="email" name="email" autocomplete="email" required /></label>
        <label>Service
          <select name="service">
            <option value="">Not sure yet</option>
            <optgroup label="Adopting talaia">
              <option>Training</option>
              <option>Audit</option>
              <option>Consulting</option>
              <option>Official certification</option>
            </optgroup>
            <optgroup label="Engineering services">
              <option>AI projects</option>
              <option>Software engineering</option>
              <option>Process optimization</option>
              <option>Forward deployed engineers</option>
            </optgroup>
          </select>
        </label>
        <label class="wide">Message <textarea name="message" rows="5" required></textarea></label>
        <div class="co-send">
          <button class="btn btn-primary" type="submit">Talk to us</button>
          <p class="co-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
        </div>
      </form>
    </div>
    <script is:inline>
      // Sends the form without leaving the page. Without JS the form
      // posts to Formspree as it is, and Formspree's own page answers.
      (() => {
        const form = document.querySelector('.co-form');
        const status = form.querySelector('.co-status');
        const button = form.querySelector('button');
        form.addEventListener('submit', async (e) => {
          e.preventDefault();
          button.disabled = true;
          status.textContent = 'Sending…';
          try {
            const res = await fetch(form.action, {
              method: 'POST', body: new FormData(form), headers: { Accept: 'application/json' },
            });
            if (!res.ok) throw new Error(res.statusText);
            form.reset();
            status.textContent = 'Thank you, your message has been sent.';
          } catch {
            status.textContent = 'The message could not be sent. Please try again.';
          } finally {
            button.disabled = false;
          }
        });
      })();
    </script>
  </section>
</Base>
